                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_426">Oxford, Oxfordshire.
                        Balliol College (Domus de Balliolo). </orgName><p>‘Liber Roberti de
                        Caldewell’ manentis apud Newark acomodatus | Fratri Thome Box loco cuius
                        habet dictus Robertus ex accomodacione fratris Thome predicti | unum par
                        Istoriarum et unum libellum parvum in nigro coopertorio qui vocatur
                        Pharetra’ (fol. 1v, s. xiv)‘Before the end of the 14th cent. the volume had
                        come to Balliol: on 4v is Libri Senece | Liber aule de Baliolo in Oxon’, and
                        the College inventory-mark 69 liber theologie is at the head of 5. During
                        the next forty years it was alienated (the Balliol ex-libris is erased), and
                        subsequently acquired by William Gray; and it was perhaps while in Gray’s
                        possession that it was in the hands of Dr. Thomas Gascoigne [. . .] One may
                        suppose that Gray took the volume with him in 1442 to Cologne, where the
                        last art. was completed, and the variants and marginalia added, and from him
                        it returned to the College.’ (Mynors, Balliol, 108)‘Liber domus de Balliolo
                        in Oxon’ | ex dono Reverendissimi in Christo patris | et domini domini
                        Willelmi Gray Eliensis episcopi’ (fol. 4v)</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">William Gray (c. 1414–1478), bishop of
                     Ely</provenance>
